CORE
main.cpp
--cpp11
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ one element: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ empty pack: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ no match: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.4\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
N5008 [temp.spec.partial.match]/2, [temp.variadic]/5: matching
probe<count_of<>> against `probe<decltype(g(declval<Args>()...)), Args...>'
deduces Args = {} from the (absent) trailing arguments; the non-deduced
first argument is then decltype(g()) = count_of<>, so the specialization
matches.  The front end selected the primary template (n == -1, bin53):
elaborate_class_template's matcher set the unmatched trailing pack to an
empty argument but did not record it as an empty pack in the template map,
so expand_type_pack_arguments left `declval<Args>()...' unexpanded and the
pattern check failed.  With one element it matched
(cpp11_pack_deduced_from_declval_arguments_in_partial_specialization/
test_type_pack_arguments.desc).  test_empty_pack_forms.desc: other pattern
forms over an empty pack.
g++ 13 and clang++ 18 accept this program, and it fails only its WRONG
assertion.
